Herpes Zoster (Shingles)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Unilateral dermatomal distribution, Grouped vesicles on erythematous base, Severe preceding neuralgic pain

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-024
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of agonizing, unilateral chest pain followed by a blistering rash, causing severe distress and fear of a cardiac event.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented an elderly patient experiencing severe, continuous burning chest pain that originated 3 days ago, now accompanied by a sudden blistering rash. Urgent assessment was required to definitively rule out atypical acute coronary syndrome, confirm the diagnosis of Herpes Zoster within the critical 72-hour therapeutic window for antivirals, and initiate immediate aggressive analgesia to manage the debilitating pain before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My right side feels like it's being continuously electrocuted and burned with a hot iron. I thought I was having a heart attack until these blisters showed up today."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports severe continuous neuralgic pain and hyperesthesia on the right lateral chest wall that began 72 hours ago, preceding any skin changes. Today, they developed a rapidly progressing, unilateral grouped vesicular eruption on a bright erythematous base. The rash strictly follows a dermatomal distribution (T5-T6) and abruptly stops at the anterior midline. The patient is experiencing generalized malaise, fatigue, and a low-grade fever. The evolution of clear vesicles into tense fluid-filled blisters is actively occurring. The intensity of the neuropathic pain is severe, causing significant shallow breathing and distress.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently immobilized by severe thoracic pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Debilitating pain limiting normal respiratory effort; risk of postherpetic neuralgia.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/92 | HR: 95 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Highly distressed, guarding the right thorax, taking shallow breaths to minimize chest wall movement.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular and respiratory examinations are normal, with clear breath sounds and no murmurs, ruling out acute cardiopulmonary pathology. On the right thorax, tracking from the posterior paraspinal region along the T5 and T6 dermatomes to the anterior sternal midline, there is a striking band of cutaneous inflammation. It features a bright erythematous base topped with multiple clusters of tense, clear grouped vesicles. The rash is strictly unilateral and does not cross the midline. Tender regional lymphadenopathy is present in the right axilla. No cranial nerve involvement; Hutchinson's sign is negative.
Environmental Observations: Clean, warm home.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current analgesics have provided relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Herpes Zoster (Shingles) - Thoracic - Status: Acute outbreak
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome / Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a classic dermatomal blistering rash, normal cardiopulmonary auscultation, and the superficial, hyperesthetic nature of the pain.

Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The strict unilateral dermatomal distribution stopping exactly at the midline, and the severe preceding neuralgic pain (contact dermatitis is highly pruritic, not deeply neuropathic).

Erysipelas / Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of grouped clear vesicles and severe neuropathic pain, rather than a uniform, hot, spreading plaque of erythema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the pathognomonic triad of severe preceding neuralgic pain, unilateral dermatomal distribution, and grouped vesicles indicative of Herpes Zoster. Triage correctly prioritized the severe chest pain in an elderly patient. Urgent on-site clinical assessment was critical to physically rule out a cardiac or pulmonary etiology for the thoracic pain, and to confirm the Zoster diagnosis within the 72-hour window where antiviral therapy is most effective at preventing prolonged postherpetic neuralgia. Prescribing potent neuropathic analgesia and systemic antivirals immediately addressed the patient's severe distress and prevented an unnecessary emergency department presentation for chest pain.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Valacyclovir 1000mg TDS for 7 days to accelerate lesion healing and reduce viral shedding. Prescribed oral Pregabalin 75mg at night for specific neuropathic pain control, alongside regular Paracetamol 1000mg QID.
Positive Behavioral Change: By starting this specific nerve pain medication tonight, you will physically calm the irritated nerves in your chest, drastically reducing the burning sensation and allowing you to breathe deeply and sleep. By leaving the blisters strictly uncovered and completely dry, you will speed up the crusting process and prevent a secondary bacterial skin infection from taking hold.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: GP review in 3-5 days to monitor pain control and assess the healing of the vesicles.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the pain becomes unmanageable despite the new medications, if you develop a severe, productive cough or shortness of breath, or if the rash spreads across your entire body (disseminated zoster), present to the Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Shingles nerve pain can be severe and may persist for weeks or even months after the rash has completely healed.
Clinical Course: The blisters will turn into pustules and crust over the next 7-10 days. The antiviral will stop new blisters from forming.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Ensure you take deep breaths despite the chest wall pain to prevent pneumonia. Seek emergency care if you become acutely short of breath.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The chickenpox virus you had as a child has reactivated in a single nerve root due to stress or age. The antiviral stops the virus, but the special nerve pain medication is required because standard painkillers cannot switch off the severe electrical burning sensation in the damaged nerve.
Actionable Steps: Do not apply sticky dressings or creams to the rash. Keep away from pregnant women and unvaccinated infants until all blisters have fully crusted over.

